This standalone example passes a function into another.
```javascript
function wave(name) {
  return `Hi, ${name}!`;
}
function welcome(makeMessage, name) {
  return makeMessage(name);
}
const greeting = welcome(wave, "Noor");
console.log(greeting);
```
It prints `Hi, Noor!`. The first argument fills makeMessage with the wave function. Inside welcome, `makeMessage(name)` calls wave with Noor. The returned string travels back through welcome to greeting.

Two callbacks let the same receiving function choose two styles.
```javascript
function wave(name) {
  return `Hi, ${name}!`;
}
function cheer(name) {
  return `Great to see you, ${name}!`;
}
function welcome(makeMessage, name) {
  return makeMessage(name);
}
console.log(welcome(wave, "Noor"));
console.log(welcome(cheer, "Noor"));
```
The outputs are `Hi, Noor!` and `Great to see you, Noor!`. Welcome has not changed; only the passed function differs. Returning text lets either message be displayed or used elsewhere.

The exercise also puts returned greetings on a page. In a browser, `document` represents the page. `document.querySelector("#g1")` finds the element with id g1; the `#` marks an id in this selector string. Assigning its `.textContent` sets its plain text. That requires the supplied HTML elements to exist. It does not treat the greeting as HTML instructions, so angle brackets in text stay text.

In a browser page containing `<p id="g1"></p>`, run:
```javascript
const output = document.querySelector("#g1");
output.textContent = "Hi, Noor!";
console.log(output.textContent);
```
This displays Hi, Noor!. querySelector receives a CSS selector string and returns the first matching element, or null if none exists. This example supplies the matching element before accessing textContent; code for an uncertain page must check for null.

