Follow a greeting callback:
```javascript
function wave(name) {
  return `Hi, ${name}!`;
}
function welcome(makeMessage, name) {
  return makeMessage(name);
}
const greeting = welcome(wave, "Noor");
console.log(greeting);
```
Output:
```text
Hi, Noor!
```
`makeMessage` receives the `wave` function. Calling `makeMessage(name)` runs `wave`, and its text is returned through `welcome`.

Change the callback while keeping `welcome` the same:
```javascript
function wave(name) {
  return `Hi, ${name}!`;
}
function cheer(name) {
  return `Great to see you, ${name}!`;
}
function welcome(makeMessage, name) {
  return makeMessage(name);
}
console.log(welcome(wave, "Noor"));
console.log(welcome(cheer, "Noor"));
```
Output:
```text
Hi, Noor!
Great to see you, Noor!
```
Each callback returns its own greeting style.
